{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nWith `timeout_method = \"thread\"` (pyproject.toml:762), a test that exceeds its pytest-timeout budget under xdist does not report `Failed: Timeout`. pytest-timeout's watchdog thread calls `os._exit(1)` in the worker. The controller sees `[gwN] node down: Not properly terminated`, the crash guard (`tests/_crash_guard.py`) prints `xdist worker=gwN terminated abnormally: Not properly terminated`, and the test is FAILED with no traceback, no stack dump, and no sign that a timeout fired. Every such incident starts as a mystery-crash investigation (e.g. uneducated-conveyable-nabarlek, subjective-forgeable-banteng, albinic-agrologic-dolphin, skipping-unamorous-massasauga).\n\n## Root cause (confirmed 2026-11-17 PST, origin/main 57d45053272, pytest-timeout 2.4.0, pytest-xdist 3.8.0, execnet as pinned)\n- pytest-timeout does print a `+++ Timeout +++` banner, any captured output, and every thread's stack before exiting. It writes them through `item.config.get_terminal_writer()` (`.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pytest_timeout.py:513-535`, `timeout_timer`).\n- In an xdist worker, that terminal writer writes to fd 1. execnet's popen bootstrap points fd 1 at `/dev/null` in every worker (`execnet/gateway_base.py:1787-1789`, `os.dup(1)` for the channel, then `dup2` of devnull onto fd 1).\n- Measured: inside a `-n 1 -s` worker, the terminal writer's file is `name=1`. `os.write(1, …)`, `sys.__stdout__` and the terminal writer all produce no output, while `os.write(2, …)` reaches the controller's stderr. So the timeout dump is written, but to `/dev/null`.\n- Reproduction: `pytest -p no:cacheprovider -n 2 -o timeout=3` on a module whose test sleeps for 30 s. The output shows `[gw0] node down: Not properly terminated`, `worker 'gw0' crashed while running '…::test_hangs'`, `1 failed, 1 passed`, and no `Timeout` text anywhere.\n\n## Approach\nWrite the timeout announcement to the worker's stderr (fd 2), the one stream xdist workers share with the controller. Keep pytest-timeout's thread method and its exit path.\n- In `tests/_crash_guard.py`, the plugin `tests/conftest.py:23` already loads in every process, add a `pytest_timeout_set_timer` hookimpl. It is `tryfirst=True, optionalhook=True`, because the hookspec exists only when pytest-timeout is installed (`pytest_timeout.py:114`, `firstresult=True`). It acts only when `settings.method == \"thread\"` and the process is an xdist worker (`hasattr(item.config, \"workerinput\")`). Otherwise it returns `None`, so pytest-timeout's own `trylast` implementation (`pytest_timeout.py:303-336`) runs unchanged. Single-process runs already show pytest-timeout's dump on the real terminal.\n- In a worker, it arms the same `threading.Timer(settings.timeout, …)` and sets `item.cancel_timeout` (cancel + join), the protocol pytest-timeout's `pytest_timeout_cancel_timer` (`pytest_timeout.py:339-344`) calls.\n- When the timer fires, the handler does the following in order:\n  1. If `pytest_timeout.is_debugging()` says a debugger is attached (and debugger detection is not disabled in the settings), it returns, matching `timeout_timer`.\n  2. It suspends global capture through the `capturemanager` plugin. This puts the worker's real fd 2 back, since fd capture redirects fd 2 to a temporary file during a test.\n  3. It writes one line to fd 2: `+++ Timeout +++ pytest-timeout expired after <N>s in <nodeid> (xdist worker <id>); the worker exits now`.\n  4. It writes every thread's stack with `faulthandler.dump_traceback(file=2, all_threads=True)`.\n  5. It calls `pytest_timeout.timeout_timer(item, settings)`, which keeps pytest-timeout's exact `os._exit(1)` behaviour.\n- The crash guard's existing `pytest_testnodedown` reporting stays as it is.\nThis fixes the class: every thread-method timeout in any xdist worker names itself and its test id in the controller's output, whichever test hangs.\n\n## Scope\n- `tests/_crash_guard.py`: add the worker-side `pytest_timeout_set_timer` hook and the timer handler described above.\n- `tests/unit/test_crash_guard.py`: add a regression test. A nested `-n 2 -p _crash_guard -o timeout=2` run of a module whose test sleeps 60 s must print a line containing both `Timeout` and that test's node id, and must exit non-zero. Also add a check that a single-process (no xdist) timeout run still shows pytest-timeout's own `Timeout` banner, so the hook stays out of the non-worker path.\n\n## Testing\n- Happy path (the bug): the new nested xdist test fails on origin/main (no `Timeout` text) and passes after the change.\n- Edge path: the single-process nested run with `-o timeout=2` still reports pytest-timeout's own banner and exits non-zero (hook returns `None` outside workers).\n- All existing `tests/unit/test_crash_guard.py` tests still pass, including the source-scan test that forbids output scraping in `_crash_guard.py`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Under xdist, a test that exceeds its pytest-timeout budget is reported in the controller output as a timeout naming the test id, not only as `Not properly terminated`. Checked by the new test in `tests/unit/test_crash_guard.py`.\n- [ ] A test reproduces the silent-crash shape and fails before the fix. Checked by running the new test against origin/main's `tests/_crash_guard.py` (fails) and against the change (passes).\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0 on the change."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1794938623812131795,"uuid":"8d6844ce-07bf-4167-9370-58db9c53f267"}